            # Article 9: Running Local LLMs Privately (Pillar, Target: >2,300 words)
            s1_extra = """The transition from cloud-hosted inference API endpoints to localized silicon execution represents a fundamental reclamation of intellectual sovereignty for researchers. When a university laboratory, financial consultancy, or medical pathology team transmits sensitive proprietary data to remote cloud servers, they introduce severe regulatory liabilities under HIPAA, GDPR, and FERPA frameworks. Cloud terms of service routinely permit anonymized telemetry harvesting, prompt caching across distributed inference nodes, and model retraining on user submissions. Operating an on-premise, air-gapped inference engine guarantees that intellectual property, confidential patient notes, and unpublished patent drafts never leave the physical silicon of the researcher's workstation."""
            s2_extra = """To maximize throughput on Apple Silicon and modern GPU architectures, understanding Unified Memory Architecture (UMA) and memory bandwidth saturation is essential. In traditional x86 workstations, data must traverse the PCIe bus between system RAM and discrete GPU VRAM, creating an 80 GB/s to 128 GB/s bottleneck. On Apple Silicon (such as the M2 Ultra or M3 Max), the CPU, GPU, and Neural Engine share a unified memory pool with memory bandwidth reaching 400 GB/s to 800 GB/s. Because autoregressive LLM inference is strictly memory-bandwidth bound during token generation (each token generated requires streaming every parameter weight through the compute cores), an M3 Max running a 4-bit quantized 70B parameter model achieves sustained generation speeds of 15 to 20 tokens per second—matching multi-thousand-dollar enterprise server rigs while consuming less than 65 watts of power."""
            s4_extra = """Furthermore, managing context window scaling requires understanding the quadratic computational complexity of multi-head self-attention. As the context window expands from 4,096 tokens to 32,768 tokens, KV-cache (Key-Value cache) memory consumption expands linearly with token count and model layer depth. For a 70B parameter model, a 32K context window can allocate over 16 GB of VRAM solely to the KV-cache. Deploying FlashAttention-2 kernels and quantized KV-cache techniques (such as 4-bit or 8-bit cache quantization in `llama.cpp`) cuts context memory overhead by 60%, preventing out-of-memory kernel panics when ingesting entire academic dissertations or voluminous legal filings."""
            s5_extra = """In enterprise research deployments where multiple lab researchers require concurrent access to the localized inference engine, running standalone Ollama instances is insufficient. Researchers must deploy **vLLM** (PagedAttention engine) behind an automated reverse proxy. vLLM dynamically partitions physical VRAM into virtual memory pages, completely eliminating memory fragmentation and enabling continuous request batching. Under continuous batching, incoming inference requests are interleaved into active generation steps, boosting multi-user throughput by 300% to 500% compared to sequential queue architectures."""

